WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.950
Dark Fate takes the Terminator franchise back
to basics, resetting the timeline back to

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.590
the end of Terminator 2.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:12.190
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video we're diving deep into Terminator:

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:16.919
Dark Fate to analyse the ending, the new timeline
and what it really means for the future of

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:17.990
the franchise.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.539
Plus I'll explain the intriguing way Arnold
Schwarzenegger's Carl connects to the T-800

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.829
from Terminator 2, and my predictions on how
the new Skynet, Legion, will be defeated in

00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:28.939
possible sequels.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:33.760
There will, of course, be spoilers for Dark
Fate and other movies in the franchise.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.289
Although it's the sixth Terminator movie,
Dark Fate is a direct sequel to Terminator

00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:39.289
2.

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.879
The events of T2 mean that Skynet no longer
exists in the new film.

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:47.660
However, the unstoppable march of artificial
intelligence means a new AI system called

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.190
Legion goes bad instead.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.899
Legion then sends a Rev-9 terminator back
in time from the future to assassinate Dani,

00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:58.280
the future leader of the resistance in this
new timeline.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.820
The Rev-9 racks up a brutal body count and
is relentless in its pursuit because it can

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.290
recover from virtually any physical damage
including being sent through a turbine in

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.770
the final showdown.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.230
As a final resort, augmented human Grace tells
Dani she must use her power source to fry

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.930
the machine's neural net processor.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.470
Removing the power source from Grace kills
her, but Dani is able to jam it into the Rev-9's

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.700
skull, short circuiting many of its functions.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.820
To finish the killer cyborg off, an injured
Carl reactivates and holds the killer cyborg

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.520
down while the power source burns them up
shutting both of them down, effectively killing

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.520
them.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.210
This ending combines the finale of Terminator
2 when the T-800 came back to life after its

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.400
secondary power source activated to rescue
John Connor from the T-1000 and the ending

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.760
of Terminator 3: Rise of the Machines where,
like Dani, the T-850 jammed its own power

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.770
source into the skull of the T-X terminator,
killing them both:

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.820
"You are terminated."

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.450
With the Rev-9 defeated, the future resistance
leader Dani has been saved.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:06.630
But a future apocalypse where the A.I. system
Legion turns against its human creators still

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.790
hasn't been avoided, so it's similar to the
first Terminator movie where John Connor's

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.620
future is saved but the apocalypse isn't prevented.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.370
In the very final scene of Dark Fate, Dani
watches a young Grace who's still alive in

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:23.070
the present day and she vows to Sarah that
she won't let Grace die for her again.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:27.650
In other words Dani wants to break the time
loop that in the future forces her to send

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.480
adult Grace back in time to die for her.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.900
This final playground scene is also a cool
homage to the iconic scene from Judgment Day

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.340
where Sarah had an apocalyptic vision of the
future and foresaw the nuclear catastrophe

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.690
that Skynet would inflict on the world.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:46.100
Dark Fate links that moment from T2 which
was what drove Sarah to prevent the rise of

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:51.120
Skynet with Dani's own decision to change
the future and stop the rise of Legion.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.280
So Dark Fate leaves the future open to change
making this new sequel to Terminator 2 a lot

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.110
more hopeful and optimistic than Terminator
3: Rise of the Machines, the original sequel

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:05.490
which took a fatalistic view that despite
everything Sarah Connor did to stop Judgment

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.120
Day in T2, Armageddon at the hands of Skynet
was unavoidable.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.840
"We stopped Judgment Day."

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.730
"You only postponed it.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.400
Judgment Day is inevitable."

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.080
That third film killed off Sarah Connor and
finished with the nuclear apocalypse that

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.400
had been averted in T2.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.300
"Our destiny was never to stop Judgment Day.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.319
It was merely to survive it..."

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:30.641
Dark Fate, by contrast, actually reveals that
there's a glimmer of hope and the future of

00:03:30.641 --> 00:03:36.350
artificial intelligence may not be as catastrophic
as seen in previous sequels after T2.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:41.020
Dark Fate's Carl is the perfect example of
how a destructive A.I. machine can actually

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.700
become a friend to humans and actively work
to do good.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.850
Carl was a T-800 that was sent back by Skynet
together with other terminators to make sure

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.540
that one of them completed the mission to
assassinate John Connor.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.500
Before Carl got to John, the events of Terminator
2 destroyed Cyberdyne and the existence of

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.040
Skynet was erased from the future.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.190
Despite this, as a machine, Carl had to follow
the mission he'd been programmed to perform.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:09.900
But after he kills John, he has no further
instructions from Skynet, so he follows his

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.550
other programmed directive which is to blend
in with human society so he can carry out

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.010
any missions covertly.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:21.239
And with over 20 years of continuous machine
learning about human society under his belt,

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:24.520
Carl has transformed into something almost
human.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.259
In fact, Carl's evolution can be seen as what
would have happened to the T-800 from Terminator

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:33.240
2 if it hadn't sacrificed itself at the end
of that movie.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.880
In T2, Uncle Bob, as John Connor nicknamed
him, learned over the course of just a few

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.169
days how to become more human, even picking
up slang:

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:42.240
"No problemo."

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.110
"Hasta la vista, baby."

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.969
Another interesting parallel between Carl
and Bob is how they both became benevolent

00:04:50.969 --> 00:04:52.360
father figures.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.620
Carl helped rescue a woman and her child from
an abusive husband and, when he talks about

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.960
how reliable he is, it echoes what Sarah thought
of the T-800 from Terminator 2.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.690
"Watching John with the machine, it was suddenly
so clear.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.969
The Terminator would never stop.

00:05:07.969 --> 00:05:09.699
It would always be there.

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:15.449
Of all the would-be fathers who came and went,
this thing, this machine, was the only one

00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:16.849
who measured up."

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:20.999
And as a measure of how human Carl has become,
notice how in Dark Fate we see the machine

00:05:20.999 --> 00:05:23.809
sitting comfortably next to the family's dog.

00:05:23.809 --> 00:05:28.900
This is in stark contrast to what was established
in the first Terminator movie, that dogs instinctively

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:34.409
bark and get nervous when a terminator is
nearby as they have an ability to detect Infiltrator

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:35.409
units.

00:05:35.409 --> 00:05:37.370
"I was dreaming about dogs."

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.339
"We use 'em to spot Terminators."

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:43.729
Carl even seems to have acquired a form of
his own free will.

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:47.979
When he meets Sarah, he tells her that because
she chose to change the future, Skynet now

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:53.699
no longer exists, meaning that he's been set
free because he has no further orders to follow.

00:05:53.699 --> 00:05:57.699
And now he's choosing to make his own autonomous
decision to protect Dani.

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:01.960
"I'm going to help you protect the girl because
I choose to."

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.659
Carl's freedom and ability to make his own
decisions also fits well the phrase "no fate

00:06:06.659 --> 00:06:11.090
but what we make" that first appeared in T2:
"The future is not set.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:14.349
There is no fate but what we make for ourselves."

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:18.740
This is echoed again in Dark Fate's flash-forward
scenes from the future where Dani rescues

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.169
Grace from attackers who are after her food.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:25.080
Dani tells the other humans that they shouldn't
be fighting amongst themselves but joining

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.490
together to fight the machines instead.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.830
When one of them says there's no point as
they can't win, Dani urges them to resist

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.830
fate, a belief that rallies people to her
cause and builds an army that is fighting

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.309
to take the world back from the machines.

00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:43.129
So, given the terrifying future we see in
Dark Fate's flash-forward scenes, what chance

00:06:43.129 --> 00:06:46.580
do Dani and Sarah have of defeating Legion
in future sequels?

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:50.180
Well, the movie sets up several interesting
possibilities.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.550
The first thing to consider is that in Dark
Fate the catastrophe happens over several

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:59.539
days, when Legion, an A.I. designed for cyber
warfare, becomes self-aware and begins its

00:06:59.539 --> 00:07:02.569
attack on humanity by turning off the power
grid.

00:07:02.569 --> 00:07:07.599
The next day, humans attempt an EMP attack
on Legion that fails, which leads to a full

00:07:07.599 --> 00:07:09.839
world war by the third day.

00:07:09.839 --> 00:07:14.430
That was eventually followed by famine which
wiped out the majority of humanity, with Legion

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.439
then hunting down any remaining human survivors.

00:07:17.439 --> 00:07:22.629
As terrible as that scenario is, it's a slower
wipe-out than nuclear annihilation, and it

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:26.830
allows for a window of time where Dani and
Sarah could attempt to prevent the catastrophe

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.529
from playing out in its entirety.

00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:33.090
Something I've already mentioned on is how
Carl is a great example of how a machine can

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.830
learn and become something almost human with
the ability to make its own decisions rather

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.389
than follow a hard-coded set of rules.

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:44.309
This is something that Sarah and Dani both
witnessed and might investigate further.

00:07:44.309 --> 00:07:49.069
For example, they could find a way to change
how Legion's machines operate so they live

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:51.699
together with humans rather than kill them.

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:56.939
In fact, there's a deleted scene from Terminator
2 that's in the Extended Cut where the T-800

00:07:56.939 --> 00:08:00.689
explains to John that his machine learning
mode is something that has to be switched

00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:01.689
on.

00:08:01.689 --> 00:08:03.800
"Can you learn stuff you haven't been programmed
with?

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.659
So you can be...
you know, more human?"

00:08:08.659 --> 00:08:12.090
"My CPU is a neural-net processor, a learning
computer.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.089
But Skynet presets the switch to 'read only'
when we are sent out alone."

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:17.179
"Doesn't want you to do too much thinking,
huh?"

00:08:17.179 --> 00:08:18.179
"No."

00:08:18.179 --> 00:08:19.399
"Can we reset the switch?"

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:24.129
Someone who might come in useful for this
is Sarah Connor's contact Major Dean, the

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:26.810
military officer who has an alliance with
Sarah.

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:31.500
Major Dean was only in Dark Fate briefly,
handing over the EMP device to Sarah.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.180
But in an interview with IGN, director Tim
Miller explained that "Dean was originally

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:41.110
in more scenes and that he's a military intelligence
officer who is wary of AI and had turned to

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.849
Sarah Connor to help him out on a few missions."

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:48.880
By the way, if you're at all confused by the
circular nature of time travel in the Terminator

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.110
franchises and the time paradoxes it creates,
well, it's basically different to the recent

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:58.231
very popular time travel movie Avengers: Endgame
in which time travel creates new [branching]

00:08:58.231 --> 00:09:00.329
timelines and alternate realities.

00:09:00.329 --> 00:09:05.079
In Dark Fate and the Terminator franchise,
time travel is a closed loop in which changing

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:07.110
the past rewrites the future.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.800
"That's not how it works."

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:09.830
"Well, that's what I heard."

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:10.830
"Wait, but who?

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:11.830
Who told you that?"

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:12.830
"Star Trek, Terminator.."

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:14.360
"Time doesn't work that way.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.440
Changing the past doesn't change the future."

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.520
Even if logically this seems to be impossible,
Dark Fate director Tim Miller has said he

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.339
prefers this type of time travel, despite
the plot holes and paradoxes it creates, because

00:09:25.339 --> 00:09:28.620
it means higher stakes for the characters.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.199
Even though Carl was damaged beyond any likely
repair in Dark Fate, it's still possible for

00:09:33.199 --> 00:09:37.870
Schwarzenegger to return in a sequel as a
brand new character, perhaps even a human

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.100
next time around.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:44.209
James Cameron has said it's unlikely Legion
would attempt to "build an Arnold T-800 in

00:09:44.209 --> 00:09:48.970
the Legion version of the future because that's
a different technological developmental pathway."

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:54.330
However, there could still be more Arnold
T-800s kicking around that Skynet sent back,

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:58.920
but what could be more interesting is if we
met the human being that Skynet based its

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.769
T-800 designs on.

00:10:00.769 --> 00:10:05.889
In a Q&amp;A in 2007, Cameron said:
"It has flashed through my mind that there

00:10:05.889 --> 00:10:07.589
has to have been a prototype.

00:10:07.589 --> 00:10:10.260
There has to have been a guy...
that they grew the Terminator from...

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.149
and that presumably was a real person at some
point.

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:17.389
And did that person have some sort of meaning
to Skynet on why they chose that one?"

00:10:17.389 --> 00:10:20.310
Now, what do you think of Terminator: Dark
Fate?

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:24.550
And do you have any theories or things you'd
like to see in potential sequels?

